# What of the user's tree sibyl's commands may read (sibylns(1)): exportfs(4)'s patterns, one
# regular expression a line, matched against paths from the tree's root, such as /lib/namespace.
# A path is there only if every + line matches it and no - line does. $home/lib/sibyl/patterns
# and a project's sibyl.patterns are read after this file, so each can only take away.
#
# /mnt, /dev, /proc, /env and /tmp are left: /lib/namespace.sibyl puts other things there. For
# instance, to keep a directory of secrets away,
#	- ^/usr/[^/]+/lib/secrets(/|$)
#
# The user's services, network and mounted trees, which would give the commands what the user has.
- ^/(srv|net|net\.alt|n)(/|$)
# sibyl's own sessions, kept in $home/lib/sibyl/n, with their trees (n.tree) and questions: one
# session's text is not another's.
- ^/usr/[^/]+/lib/sibyl/[0-9]+($|\.)
